        {
            "author_association": "NONE",
            "body": "Fair pushback, and honestly: no rigorous evidence yet. What exists is anecdotal — some LLM tooling (e.g. certain IDE/agent integrations) checks for `llms.txt` opportunistically when present, and there's a small but growing set of major docs sites that ship one (Anthropic, Stripe, Vercel, Cloudflare among them). But I can't point to a study showing it measurably changes what a model retrieves or answers for Neovim docs specifically. You're right that a general-purpose LLM can already crawl/parse the site fine without this.\n\nThe case for keeping it, if any, is that it's a static file with no maintenance burden (doesn't need to track doc changes, isn't in the build path) rather than a claim that it's currently load-bearing for AI tools. If that's not compelling to the Neovim maintainers, closing this is completely reasonable — happy to do so if you'd rather not carry an unproven convention.",
